Description: xwayland: preserve WL_POINTER_AXIS_SOURCE_FINGER into XI2
 XWayland 24.1.6 discards the Wayland pointer axis source
 (pointer_handle_axis_source is an empty function), so finger scrolling
 from a touchpad and mouse-wheel scrolling arrive at X11 clients on the
 same wheel-labelled virtual pointer. GTK therefore classifies every
 scroll as GDK_SOURCE_MOUSE and Firefox never takes its touchpad
 PanGesture/fling path (Mozilla bugs 1213601, 1564238, 1781209), even
 though smooth deltas and axis-stop already work.
 .
 This patch preserves the source semantic without inventing any motion:
 no velocity tracking, no timers, no synthetic post-lift deltas.
 .
 - The per-frame axis source is stored in the seat's pending pointer
   event and expires at dispatch like the other pending-frame state.
 - Each scroll axis tracks whether it is currently owned by an active
   FINGER gesture. A nonzero FINGER smooth axis starts/continues
   ownership; axis_stop terminates it with a zero-delta frame on the
   owning device; an explicitly non-finger source (WHEEL/CONTINUOUS/
   WHEEL_TILT) on a framed axis ends ownership so a stale FINGER
   classification can never leak into a later wheel event.
   Unknown/missing source keeps stock routing.
 - One additional XI2 slave pointer per seat ("xwayland-touchpad"),
   created/released with the seat's pointer capability via the established
   add_device/ActivateDevice/enable_device pattern, carries ONLY
   finger-owned smooth scroll valuators (same labels/scroll flags as the
   main pointer). Motion, buttons and wheel-source scroll never route
   through it, and the existing pointer device is unmodified (real mouse
   wheels stay mouse-classified).
 - XI2 scroll valuators carry running positions, not per-event deltas
   (GTK and the server's own button emulation difference consecutive
   values). The touchpad device therefore sends accumulated surface-px
   positions per axis (Portal's finger axis carries buffer-px deltas);
   stop frames repeat the position. The stock pointer keeps today's exact
   per-event values.
 - The touchpad device exposes ONLY the "libinput Tapping Enabled" XI
   property (INTEGER, 8-bit, 1 item, value 0): GTK checks this property's
   existence/shape for touchpad classification, while value 0 honestly
   reports that tap-to-click is not implemented. No XI touch class and no
   other libinput properties are added.
Author: Portal Project
Origin: Portal, patches/xwayland/0005-xwayland-preserve-finger-axis-source.patch
Forwarded: no
